speechtext:
Madam Speaker, I am glad the hon. member has referred to projections because it is worth underlining the fact that these are projections. As he knows, some of his projections were proved to be false because of developing events, and some of mine may have the same fate. I do not think it has anything to do with the honesty of the projections;
rather, it has to do with the uncertainty of projecting into the future.
I am surprised that the hon. member raises the spectre of that ill-fated mortgage plan which was brought before the House and which the government failed to pass, although it could have had it passed before its defeat last December. We told the Canadian people that we were against that, and we made it clear that we have no intention of introducing an inequitable plan of that kind.

Let us look, then, at the minister's budget. What does the minister say he is attempting to do in his budget? Let us examine some of his protestations of policy, I believe they are on page 3 under the heading "The Economic Strategy of the Government". This is what it says:
Its elements in brief are as follow:
The maintenance of government expenditures within the rate of growth in the
economy to ensure that the federal government does not take up an ever-growing proportion of the flow of income;
That is not done. That is not done, the rate of growth in the economy. The minister gets up, he is asked a question, and he talks about the trend line and the rate of growth and all that malarkey. Who is he fooling? Okay, that is one principle discarded before we even start. What next? Here is what it says:
over the period to fiscal 1983-84, a steady reduction in the government deficit and financial requirements;
Poppycock! Poppycock! It is up $3,600 million before the hon. minister even starts. I will venture anything, that there will be no reduction in the government deficit when you add in what should be there as expenditures to keep it in line with past years, there will be no reduction in expenditures or financial requirements. I read again from the budget:
the avoidance, in so far as possible, of personal and corporate tax increases;
Well, the Canadian public can thank us for that. We saved them $1.6 billion because the minister did not go forward. No wonder it was said today he changed his budget papers. He sure did. He whipped out that indexing measure after the storm we aroused to stop him trying to gouge the Canadian public.
